TECHNIQUES LEARNED FROM OTHER PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Some methods in this software were first seen, by us, in other open-source
receivers. In every such case the method was understood from its description
and re-implemented here from the principle; no code was copied, and the
other project's licence does not apply to this one. We name them because
credit is owed and because a reader may want to compare the two.

  Provenance. This project's television work began on 2026-04-29 (an
  ATSC 1.0 tuner, Software-TV-Tuner), decoded its first broadcast on
  2026-05-01, ran unattended for the first time on 2026-05-02/03, and
  decoded its first ATSC 3.0 signalling from real air on 2026-08-05 -- all
  without knowledge of OnAir. The first idea taken from OnAir is dated
  2026-11-06. Nothing before that date owes anything to it.

  OnAir  (adswill/OnAir, GPL-3.0)  https://github.com/adswill/OnAir
    Channel estimation in the delay domain for ATSC 3.0: the pilot comb's
    impulse response with the noise-only taps removed, instead of linear
    interpolation between pilots (our lab/e104_combce.py). Our version
    differs in its transform length and in refusing combs too sparse for
    the guard interval; the idea and the measured 0.5-1.5 dB cost of linear
    interpolation we learned from theirs.

    The layered (row-by-row) LDPC schedule for the ATSC 3.0 codes, as a
    way to halve the iterations a Block needs (our lab/ldpc_layered_kernel.c,
    E107): eight-lane layout, scheduling and the parity-check tables are
    ours; the choice of a layered schedule over flooding we tried because
    theirs does it.  Measured: decode time halved, a few tenths of a dB at
    the cliff.

    The two-minimum ("box-plus") correction of min-sum LDPC decoding (E110,
    ATSC3_LDPC_BOXPLUS=1).  A textbook correction; we tried it here because
    their notes said it mattered on marginal signal.  Measured: ~0.2 dB on
    strong carriers, and on marginal signal it crosses the threshold the
    interleaver lock needs -- "never locks" becomes "locks".

    Sixteen narrow lanes per vector in the layered LDPC (E130/E131, our
    lab/ldpc_layered_kernel.c): their atsc3_ldpc.cpp runs sixteen 8-bit
    lanes.  We built the 8-bit version from the min-sum rule and measured
    it losing 11-44 % of the Blocks at the cliff (no correction, a coarse
    step), so what ships is sixteen 16-bit lanes with the correction kept
    -- the lane count is the borrowed idea; the arithmetic is ours.
    Measured: no Block lost, more converged at 12 dB, 30 % less time per
    record, and the whole chain 1.87x -> 2.04x (RF33) and 2.23x -> 2.51x
    (RF25) on the 32-core box.

    Decoding every Subframe live rather than one (our lab/m17_plp1_live.py).
    Not a technique; a prompt.  They did it, so we built it.

  That is the whole of it: four techniques and one prompt, each measured
  above and in PRIOR_ART.md s13.  The receiver -- bootstrap through picture,
  the AC-4 decoder, the LDM canceller and pool, the viewer, the gates -- is
  ours, and predates our knowledge of OnAir (dates above).
